%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: ad496817cc2ac7b498b32f0fc2de6fb3, type: 3}
  m_Name: R1_06_WeightOfCooperation
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::JournalEntry
  title: "6 \xB7 Weight of Cooperation"
  pages:
  - bigIdea: "Weight is a force: W = m\xB7g. On a balance lift the heavier side goes
      down and lifts the other."
    notes: Two pans on one rope over the old pulley. Whatever one pan gains in height,
      the other loses. The brake holds them until the lever is pulled. To go UP,
      the other side must weigh more than you.
    lookCloser: "Each of you: <color=#8a5a2e>29.4 N</color>    1 kg crate: <color=#8a5a2e>29.4
      N</color>    2 kg crate: <color=#8a5a2e>58.9 N</color>\nThe lift is pushed
      by the difference: net force = W(other side) \u2212 W(your side)."
    funFact: Old mine lifts and many modern elevators hang a counterweight on the
      other end of the rope, so the motor only has to lift the difference.
    marginP1: The crates have to go DOWN for us to go up?
    marginP2: Same rope. Down there means up here.
    diagram:
      show: 1
      objectLabel: 'Crate

        2 kg'
      forces:
      - label: Weight
        newtons: 58.9
        direction: 3
        kind: 3
        unit: N
      - label: Normal
        newtons: 58.9
        direction: 2
        kind: 4
        unit: N
      caption: 'Resting on a pan: it pushes up exactly as hard as the weight pulls
        down.'
    sketch: {fileID: 0}
  - bigIdea: 'Equal weights balance. Balanced forces mean nothing changes: that''s
      equilibrium.'
    notes: 'Both of you on one pan weigh exactly as much as the big crate on the
      other. Pull the lever and... nothing. Not stuck: balanced. To lift yourselves,
      the other side must be heavier than both of you together.'
    lookCloser: "Both of you: 2 \xD7 29.4 = <color=#8a5a2e>58.9 N</color>    2 kg
      crate: <color=#8a5a2e>58.9 N</color>  \u2192 net force 0, no motion\nAdd the
      1 kg crate: 88.3 \u2212 58.9 = <color=#a26029>29.4 N</color> net, so your side
      rises."
    funFact: 'A bridge, a table, a sleeping cat: anything that isn''t speeding up
      has balanced forces on it.'
    marginP1: We pulled the lever. It just... sat there.
    marginP2: Because we weigh the same as the crate!
    diagram:
      show: 1
      objectLabel: Lift
      forces:
      - label: Crate side
        newtons: 58.9
        direction: 1
        kind: 1
        unit: N
      - label: Your side
        newtons: 58.9
        direction: 0
        kind: 1
        unit: N
      caption: 'The rope is pulled 58.9 N each way: balanced, so it stays still.'
    sketch: {fileID: 0}
  - bigIdea: 'The pan pushes up on you: the normal force. On a moving lift it changes,
      so you feel heavier or lighter.'
    notes: 'Standing still, the pan pushes up with exactly your weight. When the
      lift starts upward it must also speed you up, so it pushes harder: you feel
      heavier. As it eases to a stop at the top, you feel lighter for a moment.'
    lookCloser: "Lift: a = net force \xF7 total mass = (29.4 \u2212 4 friction) \xF7
      (3 + 2 + 2 pans) \u2248 3.6 m/s\xB2\nRiding up, each of you: N = m\xB7(g +
      a) = 1 \xD7 (29.43 + 3.6) \u2248 <color=#42612b>33 N</color>, more than your
      <color=#8a5a2e>29.4 N</color> weight."
    funFact: That heavy feeling as an elevator starts upward is the floor pushing
      you harder than gravity pulls.
    marginP1: I feel heavier. Is that a thing?
    marginP2: It's the pan pushing harder. Physics!
    diagram:
      show: 1
      objectLabel: 'You

        1 kg'
      forces:
      - label: Weight
        newtons: 29.4
        direction: 3
        kind: 3
        unit: N
      - label: Normal
        newtons: 33
        direction: 2
        kind: 4
        unit: N
      caption: "Speeding up upward: the normal force wins by m\xB7a."
    sketch: {fileID: 0}
  tip: One of you stands on the plate so the other can ride the west lift up to the
    gallery. Load BOTH crates onto the pan up there, then both of you get on the
    low pan and pull its lever. Equal weights won't move.
  sketch: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 2e7d0a61118965044b26c9285a801080, type: 3}
  sketchCaption: 'Arrows: what powers what.'
